==English==

===Etymology===
Apparently from {{inh|en|enm|*oxter}}, {{m|enm|*oxte}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ōxta}}, {{m|ang|ōhsta}}, related to {{cog|ang|ōxn|t=armpit}}, {{cog|ang|eax|t=axis, axle}}<ref>[http://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/oxter "oxter" in Merriam Webster]</ref> and {{m|ang|eaxl||shoulder}}. See also {{m|en|axis}} and {{m|en|axon}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|Scotland|Ireland}} The [[armpit]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Joyce Ulysses}}, Episode 12: ''The Cyclops'',
#*: And begob there he was passing the door with his books under his '''oxter''' and the wife beside him and Corny Kelleher with his wall eye looking in as they went past, {{...}}
#* '''1955''', {{w|Robin Jenkins}}, ''The Cone-Gatherers'', Canongate 2012, p. 90:
#*: ‘It's a small beast,’ he said. ‘I could carry it under my '''oxter'''.’

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To hug with the arms, or support by taking the arm of.

